Lesson Outline
1. Introduction to Stacks
What is a Stack?

Analogies: Stack of plates, undo/redo functionality.

LIFO Principle: Last element added is the first removed.

Generic vs. Non-Generic Stacks:

Stack<T> (type-safe) vs. Stack (legacy, stores object).

Benefits of generics: Performance, type safety.

Key Operations:

Push(T item): Add to the top.

Pop(): Remove and return the top item.

Peek(): Return the top item without removal.

Count: Get the number of elements

2. Core Code Demonstration 

Create a Stack:
Stack<string> history = new Stack<string>();

Push Elements:
history.Push("Page 1");
history.Push("Page 2");
Console.WriteLine($"Count: {history.Count}"); // Output: 2

Pop Elements:
string lastPage = history.Pop(); // Returns "Page 2"
Console.WriteLine(lastPage);

Peek and Error Handling:
if (history.Count > 0)
{
    Console.WriteLine(history.Peek()); // "Page 1"
}
else
{
    Console.WriteLine("Stack is empty!");
}

3. Hands-On Activity: Undo/Redo Simulation
Task: Build a simple text editor with undo functionality.

Steps:

Initialize two stacks: undoStack and redoStack.

Allow users to type text and "commit" changes to undoStack.

Implement "Undo" (move from undoStack to redoStack).

Stretch Goal: Add "Redo" functionality.

Stack<string> undoStack = new Stack<string>();
Stack<string> redoStack = new Stack<string>();
string currentText = "";

// Simulate user input
currentText = "Hello";
undoStack.Push(currentText);
currentText = "Hello World";
undoStack.Push(currentText);

// Undo
if (undoStack.Count > 0)
{
    redoStack.Push(currentText);
    currentText = undoStack.Pop();
    Console.WriteLine(currentText); // "Hello World"
}

4. Common Use Cases & Best Practices 
Use Cases:

Undo/redo in applications.

Parsing expressions (e.g., checking balanced parentheses (()())).

Depth-First Search (DFS) algorithms.

Best Practices:

Always check Count before Pop() or Peek().

Prefer Stack<T> over non-generic for type safety.

Avoid using stacks for random access; use List<T> instead.

5. Assessment 
Quiz:

What is the output of this code?
Stack<int> stack = new Stack<int>();
stack.Push(5);
stack.Push(10);
Console.Write(stack.Pop() + stack.Peek()); // Error! Stack has only one element after Pop().

